This is a Comparison of ImageSharp vs a C++ wrapper over Leptonica
and LibTiff to build an Image cleanup component that includes: deskew, 
decurl, black border and hole punch removal, despeckling, line removal, 
and Otsu and Sauvola thresholds. 

TLDR

Using ImageSharp, all functionality was implementable and the output was 
pixel-identical on both Windows and Linux and performed at about the same 
speed. if you're using a NET Core and a web UI or need cross platform NET 
Core support, ImageSharp can provide faster thumbnail generation and 
caching but may require a license.

Details

For each test, roughly 2400 pages were tested in 200 dpi and 300 dpi and 
consisted of real and synthetic pages. All tests were compiled for x64 
(which may be slower than their x86 equivalents) and used Claude to do 
a quick port of the wrapper, then test and time the results. 

For ImageSharp, the test environment was Ubuntu 26.04 and NET 11.

LibTiff and Leptonia C++ libraries were built on Windows using Visual 
Studio 2026. References here to "Leptonica" shall include Libtiff and all 
other dependencies. 

DFP is my own custom C++ wrapper that implements the above mentioned 
functionality plus a number of others as well as providing web support. 
DFP provides funcationality not invluded by legacy dependencies or 
needed to correct some "anomalies"; For TIFF, this included handling
stripe alignments, non-uniform pages, and bad tags, less agressive 
despeckling and smoother deskews. Where ImageSharp provides equivalent 
out-of-the-box functionality, it was used instead. The assumption is 
that apps will use some high level will exist since no comparison can
be performed against functionality that does not exist. 


