ImageSharp and leptonica are about even (within 2 ms) for deskew. One
thing to note is that ImageSharp rounds up the page dimensions and then 
trucates subsequent pages to the same dimensions so additional handling
may be required to handle the corner pixels during rotation.

Decurl

All dewarp comparisons are identical to Leptonica 1.86 on Linux and to 
1.88 and DFP on Windows. DFP provides the extended functionality, but 
ImageSharp provides a dewarp extension (not teated).

Speed: dewarping a 200 dpi page takes 58–70 ms, against 51–66 ms for 
Leptonica and DFP.

Observations

- ImageSharp read some 16 colour BMP images as 1 bit during deskew
- ImageSharp padded or rounded up a 1696 × 2175 pixel page to 1700 × 2200
  This may be intentional to avoid alignment issues later?
- ImageSharp's TIFF decoder pads smaller pages to the first page's size 
  and rejects any larger ones. The actual page size is still stored in 
  the frame but you'd have to handle this in user code. 
- ImageSharp.Web previews were 3x slower and wrote 1-bit palette PNGs 
  and ignored the configured encoder. Setting the encoder resolves this
  and eliminating the (DFP) worker cut thumbnail time from 160 ms to 20 ms. 

SUMMMARY

The overall performance difference between ImageSharp vs the orginal C++ 
Libtiff + leptonia libraries (and included dependencies) was not significant. 
ImageSharp.Web adds caching and reduced thumbnail generation time by 140 ms 
(160 vs 20). Apart from deskew, all image conversions were pixel for pixel 
identical and all tests completed. For deskew, the ImageSharp port ran faster 
but was significantly more lossy. 

The real benefit of ImageSharp, IMO, comes from cross platform portability,
simplified maintenance and web caching. However, there's no PDF generation.
That may be acceptable if you can loosely couple the conversion, but Zonal
OCR functionality will be lacking but that's likely to be true even with
most commercial products.



